                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "tldr; 100 Hours In — See You at 1,000!\n\nThis game is absolutely wild. Stationeers has been on my radar for years, and I finally picked it up during a sale. Fast-forward a few weeks, and I've somehow sunk 100 hours into it. I can confidently say **it's 100% worth full price.** The learning curve is brutal, but if you take things one system at a time and embrace the community tutorials, it becomes one of the most rewarding sandbox games I've ever played. I'd definitely recommend playing with friends, although I've spent probably half my time playing solo on our dedicated server. If you dont have friends, hit up the stationeers discord, those nerds (myself included) are always willing to struggle bus through some weird engineering adventures\n\nHour 1: My friend and I land on Mars, build a little 3x3 room, and attempt to get an airlock working. After installing the APC backwards and using the wrong airlock chip, we finally pressurize our room and get some solar panels running. We go outside to celebrate our progress, only to discover a storm has blown literally everything we own across Mars. This is also when we learn you can detach equipment from the landers. We reset. Lmao.\n\nHour 10: We've got a functioning base! Sort of. We can eat, breathe, and sleep, but our wiring looks like spaghetti, our power grid is held together by hopes and dreams, and we're constantly dying or breaking something because we didn't plan ahead. F1 and YouTube have become our best friends. We eventually realize our base is a logistical nightmare and decide to start fresh with some QOL mods.\n\nHour 50: THIS is where the game really starts to shine. We've got hydroponics, atmospheric management, suit refilling, AC, a closed-loop bathroom/shower system, and automated mining infrastructure. My friend got tired of manually emptying his mining backpack, so naturally he spent hours building a system that automatically empties it, sorts everything, processes the ores, and deposits them into storage silos. Meanwhile, I'm building dedicated service corridors to hide our wiring and piping because apparently I've become a Martian infrastructure engineer.\n\nThis is also where I think people either fall in love with Stationeers or bounce off it. Once survival is handled, the game doesn't necessarily give you objectives. You make your own. It's essentially a massive engineering sandbox where the reward is figuring out how to build something ridiculous, then figuring out how to automate it.\n\nHour 100: We're now working on automated vending machines, centralized industrial facilities, and increasingly unnecessary automation. IC10 programming adds an entirely new level of depth. Want an automated storm warning system with a countdown and sirens? You can build that. Want to spend six hours automating something that takes 30 seconds manually? Absolutely. And somehow, after 100 hours of messing with atmospheric systems, electrical grids, and industrial automation, I JUST LEARNED YOU CAN MICROWAVE A POTATO.\n\nFinal thoughts: Stationeers isn't for everyone. If you need constant objectives or a traditional endgame, you might struggle. But if you enjoy engineering, automation, problem-solving, and building increasingly elaborate systems simply because you can, this game is incredible.\n\n100 hours in, and I still feel like I've barely scratched the surface. Some of the most fun I've had gaming in years.\n\nSee you at 1,000 hours. Lol.!",
